  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale.\n    We use a multi-stage optimization algorithm that combines Frank-Wolfe with adaptive constraint handling and structured initialization. The central improvement is to use a novel convexification technique (Perron-Frobenius relaxation) that ensures better handling of the bilinear term in the objective. This addresses deterministic bottlenecks by separating the minimax objective into a nested optimization framework, reducing the risk of suboptimal local solutions.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification.\n    Representation: h \u2208 [0,1]^{400} with \u2211h_i = 200 (integral=1).\n    Objective: C5 = (2/400) * \u2211\u1d62 h_i (1 - h_{(i+k)%400}) for optimal k.\n    Constraints: 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1, \u2211h_i = 200.\n    Optimization: \n        Safe domain: h (vector) in (\u211d^400)\n        Objective: minimize (max\u2096 Sum_i h_i(1-h_{i+k})))\n        Constraint handler: Project vertex-wise using \u2211h = 200, 0\u2264h_i\u22641.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure.\n    Phase0: Initialization\n        h0 = initial_h_values (feasibility ensured by parent)\n        n = 400, dx = 0.005\n    \n    Phase1: Frank-Wolfe with adaptive penalty (300 minutes budget)\n        while time < 300:\n            Compute overlaps for all 400+399 shifts via:\n                C5 = max\u2096 sum_i h_i(1-h_{(i+k)%400}) * dx\n            Safe gradients: \u2207C5\u2096 = (dx * sum_j h_j * h_{(j+i+k)%400}) for i\n            Update slopes based on negative gradient -\u03b3 * [indicator(active shifts)]\n            Maintain \u03bd\u2208\u211d^{400} for weights\n            Project with \u03bd(i) \u2190 min(1, max(0, \u03bd(i)) after each update via:\n                h = h + residual / n_rescaled with clipping\n\n    Phase2: Structured Optimization (210 minutes)\n        For each block size b in [10,50,100]:\n            Partition indices into 400/b blocks\n            Set headers at tripled positions (i*3 % 400)\n            Use gradient projection on each header point\n            Switch between block minimization and global minimization based on residual\n\n    Phase3: Interleaved Projection (30 minutes)\n        Implement robust projection operator:\n            res = sum(h) - 200\n            If res > 0:\n                redistribution: h_i += res / n uniformly till bound\n        Add coordinate-wise analysis for gradient using multiset quantiles\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations.\n    - Frank-Wolfe uses K=45 for gradient estimation\n    - Initial block size: floor(sqrt(400))=20\n    - Structured updates every 500 iterations\n    - Projection frequency: every 3 Frank-Wolfe steps\n    - Block sensitivity: 1.5*robust covariance standard deviation\n\n5. Computation Budget and Robustness.\n    - Phase1: 300 minutes \u2192 400,000 Frank-Wolfe iterations\n    - Phase2: 210 minutes \u2192 280,000 structured updates\n    - Phase3: 30 minutes \u2192 40,000 stochastic checks\n    - Total: ~570,000 matrix operations per minute\n    - Robustness: Handle numerical instability with tridiagonal QR for gradient projections\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract.\n    Validate h:\n        i) 0\u2264h_i\u22641 for all i\n        ii) |sum(h) - 200| < 1e-7\n        iii) Re-verify C5 with np.correlate\n        iv) Fallback to projected initial if metrics diverge\n    Return format: (h_projected, C5_val, 400)",
